* Sat Aug 29 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- Provenance journal, first stage (developer-only, off by default): with
  KAME_JOURNAL set in the environment, KAME subscribes to every node and
  records what changed, on which thread and when, writing a survey to
  <AppLocalDataLocation>/journal/.  Nothing is restored and no journal file
  format exists yet; the point is to measure, on a real instrument, which
  nodes are written by the UI, by a script and by a driver -- the distinction
  the design rests on and that no flag on a node can express.  Subscription is
  the switch, so with the variable unset not one listener is attached and the
  commit path is untouched.  See doc/design/PROVENANCE.md.
- XListNodeBase::isAliasList(), replacing name tests on getTypename() that
  could never have matched: the mangled name arrives with everything up to the
  first 'X' stripped, and the alias-list subclasses do not carry the template's
  name at all.
- UI: the toolboxes and the main window now get out of the way by themselves.
  A toolbox floating at a screen edge rests shrunk to its tab column and grows
  back to full width when the pointer touches it; the main window keeps its top
  edge and rests at half height, so the menu bar and the pane tabs never move.
  Hovering a tab picks that pane, which makes a resting toolbox a menu of its
  panes.  Nothing extra is put on the screen -- the bar IS the toolbox, which is
  why its tabs keep working while it is narrow.  Neither QMdiArea nor
  QDockWidget has an auto-hide feature (Qt has never shipped one for Widgets
  docking, and Qt Quick has no docking at all), and a third-party docking
  framework was not worth the dependency for this, so it is built from the
  windows that were already there.  A window stays put, pointer or no pointer,
  while a popup is up (a combo list is its own window, outside the toolbox
  rectangle), while a mouse button is held (dragging a scrollbar), and while it
  both holds the keyboard and is the active window -- the user clicked in and is
  typing.  Both halves of that last test are needed: a pane holding a line edit
  can take focus merely by being shown, and that alone would pin it open for
  ever.  Pointer position is polled rather than tracked by enter/leave events,
  which fire for every excursion over a child widget and cannot see the gap
  between two top-level windows.  Clicking the tab of the pane already in front
  pins or unpins that window -- only that tab, so every other tab still just
  switches panes, and only when the window already held the keyboard, judged
  from the state the poll sampled, since the click itself may be what activated
  it.  "Auto-hide West/East Toolbox" and "Auto-hide Main Window" in the View
  menu are the same switch.  Three traps, each of which produced a visible
  fault: a QMdiArea's minimum size hint (~196 px, inherited from its subwindows)
  clamps the shrink, and lifting it on the area sticks while lifting it on the
  window does not -- a QDockWidget and a QMainWindow both re-derive their own
  minimum from that hint on every layout pass, so it is re-applied before each
  collapse.  Clamped, the east toolbox slid off the screen edge it clings to and
  showed its content instead of its tabs, which is what a clamp looks like when
  the anchored edge is the right one.  And a Qt::Tool window sent to the macOS
  Dock still reports isVisible() == true and isMinimized() == false, so nothing
  could tell it was gone: the toolboxes no longer carry a minimize button, and
  the View menu restores one that ends up minimized anyway.
- script: a Jupyter notebook server that died said nothing.  The tail of its
  output was already being kept for exactly this diagnosis and nothing ever
  read it, so a server that started and then exited left a half-drawn page in
  the browser -- the shell HTML is served, the script bundle that follows is
  refused -- and no message anywhere.  That is what "only the logo appears"
  looks like, and it is now reported with the last dozen lines the server
  printed.
- script: the Jupyter quick launch took the first jupyter it found on PATH,
  which says nothing about whether that one carries the package behind the
  subcommand.  Measured on this machine: of five programs found, the first
  runs notebook and lab but not qtconsole, and another runs neither console
  nor qtconsole -- so "the first" is not a safe answer and produced a
  missing-module traceback instead of a launch.  The candidates are now probed
  with `<prog> <sub> --version`, which exits non-zero exactly when the package
  cannot be imported, and the first that passes is used; the result is cached
  per session because the probe costs about a second.  When none passes, that
  is said plainly along with what to install.
  For notebook and lab the probe goes further and checks that the program's
  JupyterLab application directory carries its settings schemas, because
  importing the package proves nothing about its data files: Notebook 7 IS a
  JupyterLab application and reads a schema per plugin out of
  `<app dir>/schemas`.  With those missing the bundle loads and then every
  plugin fails to activate, and what reaches the browser is a page showing
  nothing but the Jupyter logo -- diagnosed exactly that way here, where one
  of the two jupyters on the machine had no `share/jupyter/lab` directory at
  all and was the one being picked.  That extra check is careful about where
  it applies: only for a notebook reporting version 7 or above (notebook 6 is
  a different application that reads no lab schemas, and demanding them would
  reject a good install) and it fails OPEN -- it reads a line of English CLI
  output, so a command that cannot be run or wording that changes means "no
  opinion", never "reject".  Only an application directory that is there and
  empty condemns a program.  Probes run without a console window on Windows.
- UI (macOS): choosing KAME in the Dock did not necessarily bring the main
  window forward.  AppKit orders an application's windows front keeping their
  order among themselves, so the main window stays wherever it was in the
  stack -- underneath a driver form, and always underneath the toolboxes,
  which are Qt::Tool panels marked stays-on-top.  It now asks to come forward
  when the application is activated, unless the pointer is over one of KAME's
  own windows -- clicking a driver form to raise the app is a choice of window,
  and not for KAME to overrule, while a Dock click leaves the pointer over the
  Dock.  Which window got activated cannot decide it: measured, AppKit hands
  the key back to whichever plain window held it last, by either route.  (Nor
  can `flags & Qt::Tool` tell a palette from an ordinary window: Qt::Tool is a
  composite of Popup|Dialog|Window, so that test is true of every window that
  is a window at all -- `flags & Qt::WindowType_Mask` is the comparison.)
- UI: the pane tab columns are flat.  The platform's boxed, shaded tabs with
  rotated labels were what dated the resting toolbox more than anything else
  about it: no frames now, room to breathe, and the pane in front marked by an
  accent line against the edge its column sits at (underneath, for the main
  window's tabs along the top).  Every colour is taken from the palette rather
  than written down, so it follows the platform's light/dark setting -- checked
  in both -- and the width in that same stylesheet is what sets the column's
  thickness, so the style proxy that used to widen it is gone.  Only a column
  gets that width: to tabs running along the top, `width` means each tab's
  LENGTH, and fixing it there squeezed every title out of existence, so a row
  is left to size itself around its titles.  The fold and
  unfold are quicker too, 220 ms to 170 and a snappier curve.  A fade on the
  way in was tried and dropped: a window at 0.75 opacity shows what is behind
  it, and on a bar barely wider than its tabs that reads as the tabs blinking
  out rather than as an entrance.  These windows are opaque.  (The layout
  minimum has to be lifted for the way OUT as well, not
  just the way in: a window re-derives it from the QMdiArea on every layout
  pass, so the early frames were clamped up to it -- measured, the first frame
  jumped straight from the 43 px bar to 196 px.  On the toolbox that keeps its
  right edge that showed as the tab column vanishing for an instant, the width
  being forced wide while the animation's x was still at the collapsed
  position, so the window overhung the screen edge and took its own tabs with
  it.)
- UI: an interface that appears is selected and scrolled into view in the
  Interface pane.  Bringing that pane to the front after a driver was added by
  hand only half finished the job while the row whose port has to be set was
  left for the user to find among all the others.  A .kam load ends up showing
  the last interface it added, which costs nothing: the pane is not in front
  until the load has finished anyway.
- UI: the message window's popup row could end up below the bottom of the
  screen.  That row sits under the log list and is hidden until something is
  posted, and the window is parked with its bottom on the screen edge, so
  showing it grows the window downwards.  At the size the window opens with
  (186 px) the list absorbs the row and nothing moves; but once shrunk towards
  its minimum, showing the row raises the layout minimum from 98 to 131 px and
  pushes 33 px of window -- the row carrying the message -- off the screen.
  The window now keeps its bottom edge on that screen edge whenever the row
  appears or goes, which also closes the gap the row used to leave behind.  A
  window dragged away from the edge is left where it is, and at the default
  size the whole thing is inert.
- UI: the Node Browser advertised "Supported Ruby methods" on a Python build.
  The object line above it already said Root(), but the method list under it
  was a hand-written one in Ruby's spelling (value=x, <<x, to_ary), printed
  whichever interpreter the build carried.  A Python build now prints nothing
  there: the "Supported Python methods" line that follows is introspected from
  the bound object itself, so it is both correct and complete.  A Ruby-only
  build is unchanged.
- UI: a driver created from the New Driver dialog now opens its own settings
  window, which is what one wants next after adding one by hand; loading a .kam
  deliberately still does not, since it would throw open a window for every
  driver in the file.  If the driver came with an interface, the Interface pane
  is brought to the front after that window, since its port has to be set
  before the driver can be started; a driver that talks to no hardware of its
  own leaves its own window in front instead.  An
  interface is a child node of its driver (XNode::create inserts it as one,
  which both XCharDeviceDriver and XDummyDriver rely on), so the new driver
  itself is asked rather than watching the interface list for a new arrival.
- UI: the New Driver dialog has a live search box.  Typing narrows the list of
  120 drivers as you go; every space-separated word must appear somewhere in
  the label or the type name, so "agilent dmm" finds the two of them however
  the labels happen to be worded, and a driver known by the name that appears
  in a .kam file ("KE2000") is found by that too.  While a search is in
  progress the first match is selected, so Enter creates it; an empty box goes
  back to demanding a deliberate choice.  The chosen type now travels on the
  list item instead of being recovered by counting rows into a sorted map,
  which would have picked the wrong driver the moment the list was filtered.
- UI: layout to go with it.  Both toolboxes now run from the top of the screen
  down to just above the message window, which parks at the bottom-left corner
  and keeps its top edge there, instead of stopping at half the screen height;
  the height is measured once the event loop is running, since a window asked to
  sit at the top of the screen is placed below the menu bar and its title bar
  cannot be measured until the frame exists.  The tab column is a fifth wider,
  through a QProxyStyle over CT_TabBarTab: a stylesheet can set the width but
  switches the tabs to stylesheet rendering, and larger tab icons drag each
  tab's length along with its thickness until the panes no longer fit in the
  column.  The 0.8 window opacity is gone -- a toolbox that shrinks itself out
  of the way does not need to be see-through, and text over whatever happens to
  be behind it reads worse.  The main window starts a third wider, now that the
  space between the toolboxes is its to use.  The west toolbox has the keyboard
  at startup and the east one once a .kam has finished loading, waiting for the
  loading thread because drivers, graphs and their windows go on appearing until
  it ends and would take the focus back.

* Fri Aug 28 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- Release 8.6.1.
- kamepoolalloc: frees arriving after a thread had run its own allocator
  teardown went into a destroyed object on Linux and Windows. The cold free
  path's post-teardown bypass tested `kame_page() == &g_teardown_page`, but only
  macOS ever repoints that pointer, so elsewhere the compare folded to a
  constant false and the branch was dead code: a pthread_key destructor (glibc
  runs those after the C++ thread_local ones, and kamestm registers one) pushed
  into a cross-dealloc batch whose destructor had already run, and a slot went
  back to the bitmap after its owner had re-issued it -- the same block handed
  out to two live users. It surfaced far downstream, as garbage inside a
  still-referenced STM Packet, so `transaction_dynamic_node_test` failed in five
  unrelated-looking ways, the negotiation HANG watchdog among them. The
  predicate is now portable and its flag lives in the TLS page the free path
  already loads, armed by BOTH thread-exit destructors because either can run
  first (thread_locals go in reverse order of construction, and which of the two
  a thread touches first depends on whether its first pool operation was an
  allocation or a cross-thread free). Measured on Linux x86-64 at 17 failures in
  44 control runs against 0 in 44 -- five distinct signatures against none --
  and on real -m32 i486/i586 builds, the width the fault originally reproduced
  on, at 11/20 and 12/20 against 0/20. macOS is unchanged in behaviour and one
  branch cheaper. `alloc_tsd_exclusivity_test` now asserts the exclusivity
  invariant directly, in 0.4 s, instead of waiting two minutes for the
  downstream crash. The full investigation record is deliberately not in the
  tree: see branch `claude/uaf-rc-trace`, `kamestm/tests/DYNNODE_UAF_HANDOFF.md`
  (the experiment log, and note it sits well behind master), and
  `kamepoolalloc/design/BATCH_AFTER_DESTRUCTOR.md` on branch
  `claude/great-turing-Ufao2` (the glibc ordering, the gdb captures and the A/B
  methodology lessons) -- an independent session that reached the same defect.
- script: KAME told the user the MCP server could not start, and then started
  it. The startup pre-check reported "No Python with mcp and jupyter_client was
  found, so the MCP server cannot start and the AI links above will fail" while
  the same log went on to announce the HTTP server on its port. Both messages
  were right about their own code path: launchJupyterConsole's probe strips
  KAME's Python environment before testing a candidate, but _find_python_with --
  the newer path behind the pre-check and the Pydantic AI interpreter search --
  inherited it. Under kame-msyspython.bat that environment carries
  PYTHONHOME=C:\msys64\mingw64 and MSYS2's PYTHONPATH, and every candidate
  there is a DIFFERENT interpreter from KAME's own, so a real CPython was told
  to load mingw-built C extensions it cannot open and died on `_socket`; every
  candidate failed and a perfectly good kame-mcp-venv was declared unusable. It
  now probes with PYTHONHOME / PYTHONPATH / VIRTUAL_ENV / PYTHONSTARTUP
  stripped, as the other path already did. Verified by calling the real function
  with PYTHONHOME set: it returns the venv instead of None.
- tests: the CMake test builds now configure and link under MSVC. Three things
  blocked cl before any source-level portability question arose. /utf-8 was set
  on the kamepoolalloc target alone, so every test that includes allocator_prv.h
  met the CP932 mis-decode head-on (`C2065: 'ALLOC_CHUNK_HEADER' undeclared` --
  a comment's box-drawing byte swallows the newline and hides the next #define);
  it is directory-wide now, and set before the first add_library, since
  add_compile_options reaches only targets created after it -- which is why the
  copy of the pool built from kamestm/tests still failed. The force-include of
  support_standalone.h was probed as `-include`, which cl rejects, and the
  result then silently ignored; that is not optional, because several tests open
  with `#define msecsleep(x) (x)` on line 1 and without the header going first
  that macro eats its own declaration. It is asked for as /FI on MSVC, and an
  unusable force-include is a FATAL_ERROR rather than a puzzle later. And MSVC
  emits no import library for a DLL that exports nothing, so fifteen consumers
  died on `LNK1181: cannot open input file kamepoolalloc.lib` --
  WINDOWS_EXPORT_ALL_SYMBOLS is the CMake equivalent of the
  `-Wl,--export-all-symbols` kamepoolalloc.pro already uses for MinGW. cl now
  builds 18/29 kamepoolalloc targets and 16/25 kamestm ones and every test that
  builds passes; the rest need GNU inline asm, __builtin_return_address,
  __attribute__((noinline)), pthread.h, or turn on a friend-access divergence,
  and are left for later. clang/llvm-mingw is unaffected.
- kamestm/tests: two tests could not run on Windows. transaction_priority_mixed
  used SCHED_FIFO / SCHED_RR, which MinGW does not define, though its call sites
  are already inert off Linux (os_set_policy has a `#else return false` arm and
  the OS arm then reports itself SKIPPED); only the names were missing, so they
  are defined behind #ifndef rather than excluding a test whose STM half is
  worth running anyway. And xwaitcell_test had a race of its own: each waiter
  read cells[i].gen() INSIDE its thread, so a thread scheduled after the
  parent's wake_one() asked to sleep past a generation that had already arrived,
  waited out the full five seconds and was counted as stranded -- "only 4 of 8
  sleepers were woken" under `ctest -j4`, while the same binary passed
  standalone and under six concurrent copies of itself. The primitive is not at
  fault; the generations are now snapshotted in the parent, which is also how a
  real caller uses the API.
- UI: the driver list's columns could not be resized with the mouse.  Setting
  the three sections to ResizeToContents / Fixed / Stretch had made every one of
  them non-interactive -- only QHeaderView::Interactive accepts a drag -- so this
  was the one list in KAME whose columns were frozen, and the explicit
  setColumnWidth calls above were dead for two of them.  They are Interactive
  again, with the last section still filling the width and the name column
  widening when a driver's label does not fit; that auto-fit is grow-only, so a
  width set by hand is never taken back.

* Fri Aug 21 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- script: the web UI had no model to choose. Not passing -m to a user's own
  agent (right, for chat: clai would override the model bound in their module)
  also emptied the web UI's picker, which clai builds from repeated -m. But a
  module that calls Agent.to_web(models=...) already declares the roster its
  author wants, and `clai web` cannot see it because it takes an Agent, not an
  app. So the picker now records that app too, and the web link serves it with
  the venv's uvicorn -- the switcher then works exactly as written. Modules
  without one still go through clai, where KAME_PYAI_MODEL may now list several
  models (comma or space separated) to populate the picker.
- build: the two scripting languages had their failure modes backwards. A
  missing Ruby stopped the build (error() on Linux and MSVC) while a missing
  Python was silent on macOS and Windows and a mere message on Linux -- so the
  deprecated language could block a build that never wanted it, and the
  supported one could go missing without a word, leaving a KAME with no
  scripting, no Jupyter and no MCP, discovered at runtime. Swapped: no Python
  is now an error naming `CONFIG+=no_python` as the way to mean it, and no Ruby
  drops the interpreter with a message (`CONFIG+=no_ruby` to silence). macOS
  gained the existence test its framework arm never had. Verified by generating
  Makefiles: default has both, no_python clears USE_PYBIND11 and the
  xpythonsupport.o rule, no_ruby clears USE_RUBY and xrubysupport.o, and the
  auto-disable path was checked separately -- SOURCES/HEADERS -= removes
  exactly the Ruby entries and leaves the rest.
- script: the Ruby line shell says it is deprecated, in its greeting, where it
  cannot be missed: planned OFF by default from 9.0, droppable today with
  `qmake CONFIG+=no_ruby`, and port a .seq by asking the AI assistant, whose
  mapping is in kame_api. The .kam format is unaffected either way.
- script: the Python banner warns when nothing on the machine can run the MCP
  server, beside the links that would then fail. The detection already existed
  but printed to stderr only, which in a GUI session is nowhere. Probed on a
  daemon thread so the banner is never delayed, and the candidate list mirrors
  the real search -- the interpreter behind Jupyter first, then a
  kame-mcp-venv -- because a false alarm on a working setup would be worse than
  silence. Verified both ways: silent against a venv that has the packages,
  and it reports with the setup URL when nothing does.
- build: `qmake CONFIG+=no_ruby` builds without the Ruby interpreter. Ruby had
  no switch at all, unlike USE_PYBIND11: XRuby was constructed unconditionally
  and Linux refused to configure without ruby-dev ("KAME compiles
  script/xrubysupport.cpp unconditionally"). Default is unchanged -- this only
  makes the question measurable, since deciding to drop Ruby needs a build that
  can be tried. What is NOT affected is the .kam format: XRubyWriter merely
  writes text, needs no libruby and is always built, and .kam files already load
  through the Python loader whenever USE_PYBIND11 is set. What goes away is
  running .rb files and the Ruby line shell. Verified by generating both
  Makefiles: with no_ruby there is no compile rule for xrubysupport.o, no
  USE_RUBY define and no libruby in LIBS, while xrubywriter is untouched; and
  measure.cpp / kame.cpp / xrubywriter.cpp syntax-check both ways.
  Motivation, for the record: libruby installs a SIGSEGV handler, which is what
  turned a segfault in Python teardown into an endless spin rather than a crash
  -- a cost paid on every platform for an interpreter most installations no
  longer use.
- script: setup-time failures now carry a link to the setup instructions.
  Someone getting MCP working for the first time cannot consult the manual: it
  is served THROUGH MCP, so it is unreachable until the thing they are fixing
  works, and by then they no longer need it. The only surface they have is
  KAME's own message and the plugin launcher's stderr, so the URL travels in
  those.
- script: the web link opens the browser. `clai web` prints its URL and waits,
  and nothing opened it, so choosing "web" looked like it had merely opened a
  terminal. KAME picks a free port, passes --host/--port, and opens
  http://127.0.0.1:<port> once something answers there -- from a daemon thread,
  because kame: link handlers run on the main thread and blocking it would
  freeze the GUI while the server starts. The terminal window stays: it is
  where the server lives, and where its errors are legible.
- script: -m is passed only to the agent KAME ships. clai overrides
  unconditionally when -m is present (`if agent.model is None or
  model_arg_set`), and the shipped agent binds no model precisely so clai can
  fill it in -- but an agent the user wrote usually binds its own, and passing
  -m then silently replaced the model chosen in their module, which is the
  opposite of the point of picking one. The launch log now names the agent and
  says which of the two supplied the model.
- script: choosing your own Pydantic AI agent is a file dialog, not an
  environment variable -- a GUI application should not require one exported
  before launch. The Script pane's Pydantic AI row gains an agent picker: it
  accepts a .py module or a .yml/.json spec, and for a module it asks the venv's
  interpreter whether the file really exposes a pydantic_ai.Agent and under
  which name, rather than assuming `agent` and failing later inside clai with
  nothing to go on. The choice is remembered in ~/.kame_pyai_agent, the module
  runs from its own directory so its imports resolve, and Cancel returns to the
  agent KAME ships. KAME_PYAI_AGENT still works for scripted setups. The point
  is that KAME owns the MCP endpoint and nothing else: an agent that also wants
  a Coder capability, run-spanning memory, web search or a roster of models to
  switch between belongs in the user's own module, needing only KAME's toolset
  added to it as one capability.
- kame/script: KAME "hung" on quit instead of exiting. The stack was identical
  across samples taken seconds apart while CPU kept climbing: exit() ->
  __cxa_finalize_ranges -> _Py_Dealloc -> _sigtramp -> libruby's sigsegv ->
  check_stack_overflow. So it was not a hang at all -- a segfault during Python
  teardown, caught by Ruby's SIGSEGV handler (installed because KAME embeds
  Ruby as well), which then spun there rather than dying. XPython::bind was a
  static KAMEPyBind, so its destructor ran in that finalisation phase holding
  two things that must not be touched then: a pybind11::module_, whose release
  DECREFs a Python object when the interpreter can no longer dealloc one, and
  maps of std::function whose lambdas live in driver-module dylibs that may
  already be unloaded -- the static-destruction-order hazard already flagged in
  CLAUDE.md, now with a symptom attached. It is a reference to a never-freed
  object now; the OS reclaims it. Suspicion fell first on a leftover async job,
  but no worker thread existed in the sample: the only other Python threads were
  ipykernel's _watch_pipe_fd pair, parked in PyThread_hang_thread, which is
  CPython 3.14 doing what it should to daemon threads after finalisation starts.
- mcp: an async job that starves the kernel's message loop used to be
  unstoppable and unobservable, because get_result and stop_job were both
  implemented by executing code ON the kernel -- the one thing that stops
  working exactly then. Reported from a temperature-monitoring loop where the
  GUI stayed responsive (so this was Python-side starvation, not an STM stall)
  and get_result, stop_job, tree and execute_code all went silent. The worker
  thread keeps running in that state, so each mcp_checkpoint() now also writes
  ~/.kame_mcp_log/jobs/<job_id>.json and checks for a <job_id>.stop marker
  that stop_job drops from the SERVER process, before it tries the kernel at
  all. Both tools fall back to the files when the kernel does not answer.
  Verified with no kernel in the loop: a 200-iteration job stops within one
  checkpoint of the marker appearing, and its progress is readable throughout.
  Note this is recoverability, not the root cause: the starvation itself is
  still open -- Timer.start() only polls shell_stream.flush() after on_timer()
  returns, so anything that blocks inside on_timer() stops all message
  handling. The async mechanism alone does not do it (a Snapshot+sleep loop
  leaves the kernel answering normally with no drivers loaded).
- mcp 2.x is supported alongside 1.x, so `pip install mcp` will be the right
  instruction from the next release. 2.0 renamed the class and moved the module
  (mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP -> mcp.server.MCPServer) and moved transport
  options out of settings into run(); the server now picks whichever line it
  finds. Verified against 1.29.0 and 2.0.0: both list all eleven tools with
  their annotations over stdio, and both bind the streamable-http transport.
  Two details a plain import swap would have missed -- Image lives at
  mcp.server.mcpserver.Image on 2.x and is NOT re-exported from mcp.server,
  and the host/port handling is exactly inverted (1.x: settings, run() takes
  only transport/mount_path; 2.x: run() kwargs, gone from settings), which is
  why _bind() became _serve(). mcp.types.ToolAnnotations and the annotations=
  keyword are unchanged. The interpreter probes accept either line -- requiring
  the 1.x module would have rejected a perfectly good `pip install mcp` -- and
  the plugin launchers' `import mcp` check, which would pass a package too
  broken to serve, now reaches the server class itself. That probe has to stay
  a single line: \n inside sh single quotes is a literal backslash-n, so a
  multi-line -c is a SyntaxError that rejects every candidate.

* Fri Aug 21 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- Release 8.6.
- script: an MCP async job could not touch the per-thread script context.
  execute_code_async runs the code in a daemon thread, and TLS was a plain
  threading.local(), so every field was unset there and a read raised
  `AttributeError: '_thread._local' object has no attribute 'xscrthread'`.
  Reproduced first, which narrowed it usefully: Root(), Snapshot, Transaction,
  print() and sleep() all work on that thread already -- only the direct TLS
  reads failed. TLS is now a threading.local subclass whose __init__ (called
  once per thread that touches it) defaults xscrthread / logfile / cell_status
  to None, so a non-script thread reads None instead of raising, and no thread
  can see another's context. That is also what the hasattr/getattr guards
  scattered through this file were working around. kame_python_api says what an
  async job may rely on.
- Windows: MCP did not work at all, for four independent reasons, and each hid
  the next. Nothing ever deployed kame.pro's scriptfile.files there -- they sit
  in DISTFILES, which copies nothing, while the macOS bundle and the Linux
  QMAKE_POST_LINK do the real work -- so kame.exe ran against a resources\ dir
  holding only whatever had once been hand-copied, and the server launch failed
  with `can't open file '...\Resources\kame_mcp_server.py'`. A win32
  QMAKE_POST_LINK now runs tools/deploy_scripts.bat at link time and
  tools/mkzip.bat calls the same script, so a build tree and a release get an
  identical set; the recipe needs system_path() rather than shell_path(),
  because with MSYS on PATH qmake decides the make shell is sh and emits
  /C/Users/... which a recipe run under `mingw32-make SHELL=cmd.exe` cannot
  execute. Second, `pip install mcp` now serves 2.0.0, which dropped
  mcp.server.fastmcp: `import mcp` still succeeds, so the only symptom is a
  ModuleNotFoundError on the submodule -- pinned to 1.x explicitly. Third,
  kame-msyspython.bat exports PYTHONHOME=C:\msys64\mingw64 and MSYS2's
  PYTHONPATH, and both the interpreter probe and the server launch inherited
  os.environ verbatim, so a real CPython was told to load mingw-built C
  extensions it cannot open (`No module named '_socket'`, or exit 103 through a
  uv trampoline). Worse, the probe failed for that same reason, so the venv
  looked unusable and the fallback picked it anyway. Both now run with
  PYTHONHOME / PYTHONPATH / VIRTUAL_ENV / PYTHONSTARTUP stripped. Fourth,
  FastMCP.run(host=, port=) raises TypeError in mcp 1.x, where those are
  constructor/settings values, so all three HTTP call sites could never bring
  the server up even with a clean environment.
- script: the Windows Claude and Codex desktop apps are MSIX packages -- not on
  PATH, no executable under Program Files, and absent from both App Paths and
  the Uninstall registry -- so `start "" Claude` opened nothing and the Codex
  link reported its CLI missing on a machine where the app was installed. They
  are launched by AppUserModelID through the AppsFolder shell namespace, with
  the id resolved from the OS rather than hardcoded (Claude_pzs8sxrjxfjjc!Claude,
  OpenAI.Codex_2p2nqsd0c76g0!App); a plain-exe install has no AUMID and keeps
  the old route. The session-scoped -c MCP overrides cannot ride along that
  path, which the log line now says. The same packaging redirects %APPDATA%
  into Packages\<pkg>\LocalCache, which is why an interpreter installed from
  inside such a container resolves there and not for KAME.
- plugin launcher: both launchers now walk up for kame-mcp-venv, the same
  interpreter KAME's own probe prefers, ahead of the system ones. They searched
  only KAME_MCP_PYTHON, `py -3`, `python` and `python3` -- on Windows typically
  the Microsoft Store alias stub or an MSYS2 python that cannot host mcp at all
  -- so an installed plugin reported "no Python with 'mcp'" while KAME was
  happily using the venv.
- script: an MCP client config that exists but is empty is read as {} rather
  than raising. Antigravity CLI leaves a 0-byte ~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json
  until it first writes one, and json.load() rejects that. Malformed-but-
  non-empty still raises: that is somebody's real configuration. The directory
  is created when absent, which would have failed one step later.
- tools: mkzip.bat's cleanup never ran -- the trailing lines used `remove`,
  which is not a cmd command, so qtdir.txt and kame.log shipped in every
  release; and __pycache__ is nested, while the single rmdir matched only the
  top level (12 -> 0 on the current release folder). findstr has no alternation
  and its `*` repeats only the preceding element, so the Qt-version filter needs
  `6.[5-9]` *and* `6.[1-9][0-9]`: without the second, kame-msyspython.bat
  silently stopped finding Qt at 6.10 while kame.bat still did.

- macOS: the GUI held a full core with nothing running. main() does not use
  app.exec() on this platform -- since 2018 it runs its own
  while(form->running()) { pool; app.processEvents(); } so each pass can be
  wrapped in an autorelease pool -- and processEvents() defaults to AllEvents,
  which does NOT include WaitForMoreEvents. Qt computes
  canWait &= (flags & WaitForMoreEvents), so that loop could never block: it
  was a busy-wait from the day it was written. It did not show because
  processSignals() slept 5 ms per idle pass and that sleep paced the loop as a
  side effect; moving the pacing to the timer interval (right on Linux, where
  app.exec() asks to wait) left macOS with nothing holding it back. Sampling
  put 92% of the main thread in running states and 44% of it inside the Cocoa
  dispatcher re-arming its CFRunLoop timer -- work whose whole purpose is to
  prepare a sleep that never came. A bare QApplication whose only timer is a
  do-nothing zero-interval one reproduces the same two frames at 85% of a core.
  The loop now asks to wait, and the signal pump's timer is never 0: 0 means
  "never sleep", not "soon".
- kame/xscheduler: SignalBuffer::synchronize() reported "busy" for two
  unrelated states and the single bool could not tell them apart -- a real
  backlog left by the 30 ms drain budget, where returning at once is exactly
  right, and an empty queue with an event parked in the skipped queue waiting
  out its listener's delay_ms(), where nothing can progress before the delay
  expires and polling is pure spin. hasImmediateEvents() separates them, so
  the aggressive path keeps its 0 ms and the deadline that has not arrived is
  simply waited out.
- script: the Jupyter server KAME launches now dies with KAME. finish() only
  runs on a clean kernel-loop exit, so a crash or a kill left the server alive
  holding its port -- seven had accumulated in eleven hours, each pushing the
  next launch to 8889, 8890, ... The decisive check therefore lives in the
  server: a daemon thread watches the KAME pid and sends itself SIGTERM when it
  goes (0.09 s, measured), taking the kernels it started with it. It must not
  probe with os.kill(pid, 0) on Windows, where Python maps every signal other
  than CTRL_C_EVENT/CTRL_BREAK_EVENT onto TerminateProcess -- the probe would
  kill KAME. Three parent-side defects went with it: only the last launch was
  remembered (a second click orphaned the first for good), terminate() was
  called twice with nothing checking that the server had gone, and stdout was a
  PIPE nobody read after the 0.5 s launch check, so the server would block once
  the ~64 KB buffer filled. Clicking the link again now reopens the running
  server.
- script: MCP clients that KAME did not launch can be registered permanently
  from the Script pane. The entry names the plugin's stdio launcher, not the
  HTTP URL, so it survives KAME restarts and is inert while KAME is closed.
  Each client is reached the way it provides: `codex mcp add`, `agy mcp add`
  (Antigravity CLI -- and its CLI records "disabled": false beside the command,
  which a hand-built entry would omit, which is the argument for using it), and
  an additive edit of claude_desktop_config.json for Claude Desktop, which
  offers neither. Bionic / LM Studio need nothing: a project opened on the
  notebook workspace reads the .mcp.json KAME already writes there. Two clicks,
  not one -- the first reports every target path and the old and new entry, and
  only the second writes.
- script: the Pydantic AI links hand KAME's agent to the user's own clai
  (`clai -a kame_pydantic_ai:agent`) instead of running the script with a model
  KAME had to ask for. Imported as a module, kame_pydantic_ai exposes `agent`
  with no model bound and only KAME's MCP toolset and safety instructions, and
  clai fills the model in from -m or its own default -- so provider, keys and
  defaults stay in the setup the user already has.
- script: the venv dialog accepts the folder people actually pick. uv, poetry
  and pdm keep the interpreter in a .venv that macOS's file dialog does not
  show, so demanding <picked>/bin/python turned the natural choice into an
  error; .venv/, venv/ and env/ inside it, a bin/ directory and the interpreter
  itself are all accepted now, and every candidate is probed rather than the
  first assumed. A venv under Documents that fails with EPERM is reported as
  what it is -- macOS privacy, not a missing package.
- mcp: tracebacks reach the client without IPython's colour escapes, which were
  43% of the payload on the sample measured and unrenderable by the reader.
  kame_python_api gains the async polling shape and, for driver cleanup, the
  `finally` idiom -- explicitly NOT a blanket context manager, because
  unconditional teardown is wrong for a controller mid-ramp, a magnet at field
  or a gate mid-sequence.

* Wed Aug 19 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- Release 8.5.
- Linux: first run against real instrument hardware. The Thamway FX2/FX3 USB
  path had never been exercised on Linux before, and it produced four crashes
  in one session; all four are fixed here, and three of them were the same
  bug. Taking &v[0] on a std::vector whose size is 0 is undefined, but the
  capacity is allocated, so on macOS and Windows it returns exactly the
  address the caller wanted and the code had "worked" for years. Qt6's headers
  define _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS whenever QT_NO_DEBUG is absent, so libstdc++
  checks operator[] and aborts -- which is why a debug build on Linux was the
  first thing to see it. Every site of that shape across the DSO, NMR and ODMR
  modules moved to .data(), which is defined for an empty vector and yields
  the same address; they were found by enumerating the containers rather than
  the syntax. Two ways the vectors are
  legitimately empty at those points: stopAcquision() reserve()s each ring
  chunk and then clear()s it, and visualize() runs even when analyze() bailed
  out with an XSkippedRecordError.
- charinterface: four defects on the libusb async path, found from a SIGSEGV
  inside libusb_cancel_transfer in that same session. AsyncIO::abort()
  cancelled a transfer without checking the device was still open, and held no
  reference with which to check -- it now carries one. waitFor()'s watchdog
  used a 6 s deadline while the transfers are issued with 10 s, so libusb
  could never report LIBUSB_TRANSFER_TIMED_OUT and the clean outcome the code
  below already handled was dead: the deadline now comes from
  transfer->timeout. Past the deadline the loop re-issued the cancel on every
  iteration; it now cancels once, grants a grace period, then raises a
  catchable error. close() cleared its handle only after libusb_close() had
  freed it, leaving every !handle guard in the file a window to walk through.
- kame/70-kame.rules: TAG+="uaccess" alone grants access only while the user
  holds an active local seat, so a measurement host driven over SSH, from cron
  or from a systemd unit failed every libusb open with LIBUSB_ERROR_ACCESS
  although the same user succeeded from the console a moment earlier.
  GROUP="plugdev", MODE="0660" alongside uaccess on all ten devices.
  INSTALL.linux gains the usermod step, the re-plug requirement and the
  ls/getfacl checks, because nothing USB works until the rules are installed.
- pulser: two operator-precedence bugs and a unit mismatch. '!pw2__/2' parses
  as '(!pw2__)/2' == 0, so the "no pi pulse follows, close the PreGate now"
  branch was dead: with PW2 == 0 and a short Tau the amplifier gate stayed
  high across the rest of the sequence, and in ODMR mode it was never released
  inside the echo loop either. Both fixes strictly close the gate earlier.
  Separately, ASWSetup is [ms] while Tau is [us], so its clamp compared the
  two directly -- about 1000x too permissive, which is why it had never once
  fired. Correcting the units made it fire, and that proved worse than leaving
  it wrong: the clamp writes a node onPulseChanged itself listens to, without
  the re-entry guard and the return that its neighbour two lines below
  carries, so every firing re-entered the callback. Nor did it settle -- the
  value written is not a fixed point of the rounding the next invocation reads
  it back through, so the comparison can stay true indefinitely, and one click
  on Output recursed 232 levels and took the stack with it. The clamp is gone.
  ASWSetup is no longer bounded by Tau, which is what every release so far
  has shipped.
- pulser: refuse pulse widths that exceed Tau rather than playing them.
  Nothing checked that the RF pulses in a sequence are disjoint, so
  PW1 = PW2 = 6 us against Tau = 0.6 us was accepted: twenty pi pulses 600
  ticks wide on a 120-tick spacing, overlapping five deep. Their QAM pulse
  indices then merge into a single ~2400-tick span, while the waveform behind
  that index is built for one 600-tick pulse, and the back-end walks off the
  end of it. createRelPatListNMRPulser now raises an XRecordError, which
  blanks the ports through the record's time-stamp gate. The Thamway back-end's
  own guard against the overrun was an assert, which NDEBUG drops -- so the
  release build read past the end of the vector instead of stopping. It
  throws now, and remains as a backstop, since on its own it only fires when
  the pulse width sits near an integer multiple of the spacing.
- pulser: blank the output when the record has no valid time stamp.
  visualize() emitted the output unconditionally, so a pattern-generation
  failure left the hardware running the pattern from before the failure: RF
  kept being emitted from a setup KAME had already rejected, and the UI
  advertised parameters the hardware was not playing. Both changeOutput()
  calls are now gated on the stamp. XSkippedRecordError paths are unaffected.
- optics: the Euresys eGrabber probe sat inside optics.pro's `unix { }` block
  along with libdc1394 and libusb. For those two that is a library fact; for
  eGrabber it was an accident, and it left a Windows build with no camera
  driver of any kind, while the ODMR imaging, FM tracker and 2-D analysis
  drivers all build there. The probe now has a per-platform default and still
  honours EGENTL_HOME. The dc1394 probe also accepts `libdc1394-25`.
- The ODMR driver is built by default.
- kame,modules: the OS elevation is now asked for directly instead of through
  an STM tier. Five acquisition threads called setCurrentPriorityMode(HIGHEST)
  outside AcquisitionPriority; three of them never enter the STM at all, so
  what they were buying was the Windows arm that mapped HIGHEST to
  THREAD_PRIORITY_TIME_CRITICAL -- and when that arm was removed, those calls
  became no-ops and the threads silently lost their priority. The two that do
  commit sat at STM HIGHEST outside the reach of the 2026-07-31 verdict. The
  OS half is now an RAII wrapper, and the STM half of AcquisitionPriority,
  which was dead, is gone.
- Linux is a supported platform from 8.5: the "unsupported" label is dropped
  from README and INSTALL.linux. Since the 2026-07 port the tree compiles
  warning-clean, loads all 43 driver modules, runs both script engines,
  renders its OpenGL graphs on a real X11 server, builds the usermode NI
  USB-GPIB driver, and is the host the realtime campaign measures on. Two
  facts stay as facts rather than disclaimers: there is no packaged Linux
  binary yet, and -- as of this release -- the hardware run above is the
  first, so reports naming the driver and the device are wanted.
- script: one-click Codex / codex-fugu launch alongside the existing Claude
  entries, wired to the same throwaway .mcp.json KAME already writes, so the
  CLI is pointed at exactly the server this KAME started. On macOS and Linux
  the registration is an ephemeral, session-scoped `-c mcp_servers.kame.*`
  override, so ~/.codex/config.toml is never touched; Windows delegates to
  `codex mcp add` so cmd.exe need not quote a TOML array. The bearer token
  never appears in argv -- where any local user could read it out of ps for
  the life of the terminal -- but in a 0700 launch script that deletes itself
  on first read.
- STM: the tail on a PREEMPT_RT host is contention, not composability, and the
  campaign that established that is summarised in the Aug 10 entry below;
  since then Rule 0c (a lower-priority tagger never overwrites a validated
  HIGHEST tag) took slow commits from 62 to 15 per 900 s at 5.4 sigma, HIGHEST
  now tags from retry 0 so a bundle no longer leaves its children unstamped,
  and a 30-minute soak holds the 3L retry bound to within one in 115 M
  commits. Two published results were retracted on re-measurement and the
  reasons kept: "RT fast privilege makes the tail worse" did not replicate,
  and the no_tags result tracked run order rather than the binary.
  KAME_STM_RT_FAST_PRIV and the OS-scheduler probe are deleted outright.
- kamestm: diagnostic counters are pointer-width -- the STM core would not
  link on i486, where a 64-bit atomic becomes a locked libatomic call and one
  in a free path destroys lock-freedom.

* Mon Aug 10 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- STM realtime, measured end to end on a PREEMPT_RT host (i5-7500, isolated
  core, SCHED_FIFO, per-thread pinning, PM-QoS): a HIGHEST 5-node commit with
  peers writing into the same subtree runs p50 896 ns / MAX 21.9 us; with no
  peer on its subtree, p50 448 ns / MAX 1.06 us. NORMAL under the 20 ms wait
  budget lands at MAX 20.03 ms, i.e. the budget. Contention -- not the number
  of nodes a commit spans -- is what sets the tail: 21x in MAX between those
  two rows, while 17x the nodes moves the worst case only 1.6x (p50 is linear,
  439 ns + 94.5 ns per node). The lever is topological: a root-scope Snapshot
  or Transaction bundles every subtree beneath it, so keep other threads, and
  root-scope operations above all, off the deadline-bearing subtree.
- STM: the livelock probe called std::thread::hardware_concurrency() on every
  tick. On Linux/glibc that is get_nprocs(), i.e. an openat + read + close of
  /sys/devices/system/cpu/online each time -- three syscalls through PTI+IBRS
  inside the negotiation of a realtime commit, and the probe sits above the
  round loop that HIGHEST breaks out of, so HIGHEST paid them too. Now cached
  per process. Slow commits (>= 15 us) fell 879 -> 5 in a 60 s contended arm,
  MAX 40.8 -> 21.9 us, p50 and throughput unchanged; on macOS the same fix is
  worth ~16 % of 3level_mixed throughput. It was found with Intel PT after
  five other instruments missed it: fault and context-switch counters do not
  see a syscall, a cycles profile smeared it to 0.27 % of samples, the STM's
  own counters account sleeps and CAS but not kernel entries, and strace is
  not an option on the thread under test.
- STM: KAME_STM_RT_FAST_PRIV (default off) fires the privilege claim early --
  at N retries -- for a HIGHEST transaction on SCHED_FIFO/RR. Measured, it
  makes the tail WORSE (MAX 27.6 -> 34.5 us) and the reason is worth the knob
  existing: privilege blocks a peer at its next negotiation entry and can do
  nothing about CASes already in flight, so cutting the snapshot loop short
  converts cheap rebuild conflicts into commit-CAS losses. The organic gate's
  tags_owned == tags_total condition, which the fast path bypasses, is
  therefore an anti-leak filter rather than conservatism -- owning every tag
  means the storm has drained and the commit can actually win. The knob keeps
  its own measurement in a comment so the next person does not re-run it.
- atomic_smart_ptr: the exponential-backoff knob has never been reachable --
  BACKOFF_IN_ATOMIC_SMART_PTR is always defined (0 by default) and all four
  use sites guarded on #ifndef, so every one took the no-op branch and
  -DBACKOFF_IN_ATOMIC_SMART_PTR=4 changed nothing. Guards corrected to
  #if > 0; the default remains 0, so no shipped behaviour changes.
- kamestm/README: the realtime section is now 83 lines of contract and
  conclusions (from 420 during the campaign). tests/latency_floor plus
  tests/with_pmqos measure the host's own floor -- 67.9 us with no isolation,
  17.0 us with isolcpus/nohz_full, 219 ns with PM-QoS on top -- because a
  commit latency read against the wrong floor mis-attributes the machine to
  the STM, which happened twice during this campaign. The investigation
  record lives in design/RT_READINESS.md and in the test headers.

* Sat Aug 08 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- fast_vector<T,N>: shrink_to_fit() ran on the INACTIVE union member. The
  fixed inline array and the std::vector are union'd, and the is_fixed() test
  was inverted -- so in inline mode it read m_array's bytes as a vector's
  three pointers and reallocated through them. Talker::connect and
  Talker::disconnect call it on every connect and disconnect, and the listener
  list is inline for the usual case of four listeners or fewer, so this was
  live on every platform; whether it corrupted anything depended on the stale
  bytes, which is why it surfaced as an intermittent SIGSEGV at window close
  (XMeasure::terminate_all -> Talker::disconnect) rather than a reliable one.
  Two adjacent defects of the same class fixed with it: begin()/end() used
  out-of-range operator[] subscripts in heap mode, and erase() dereferenced
  end() when the last element went. New kamestm/tests/fast_vector_test.
- STM: a budgeted wait is never armed to land ON the deadline any more. The
  ~200 us of overshoot recorded for every budget size was not the STM and not
  the budget-exempt wait behind a privileged peer (instrumentation found zero
  exempt rounds in 17,274 slow commits) -- it was the timed wait's own
  wake-up: one cell.wait() asked for 198 us returned 696 us later with 6 us
  of STM work in the whole commit. The scheduling class dominates that cost
  and a PM-QoS hold buys its remaining 6x only on top of FIFO. Every budgeted
  sleep now stops KAME_NEG_SPIN_TAIL_US (300 us) short of the deadline and
  polls the remainder, which also notices the blocker clearing immediately:
  MAX - budget 122 -> 7.1 us at 20 ms, 3.0 us in the full configuration.
  Unbudgeted callers execute the pre-existing code. Keep budgets well above
  the reserve: at or below it the committer never sleeps and the deferrable
  tiers starve (-94 % / -98 % measured at a 200 us budget).
- XWaitCell parks on futex(FUTEX_WAIT_PRIVATE) on Linux, matching the
  __ulock_wait path already used on macOS, so the negotiation route has no
  mutex on either. The portable fallback's std::mutex has no priority
  inheritance, which is a real hazard under a scheduler that enforces
  priority; it remains for other platforms and xwaitcell_test covers all
  three backends.
- timeStamp() reads CNTVCT_EL0 on aarch64 instead of falling through to
  XTime::now(), whose resolution is one microsecond. Apple Silicon and any
  ARM realtime target had the library's lowest-level timing primitive 1000x
  coarser than x86 with nothing saying so.

