- Linux: file dialogs work properly. XFilePathConnector passed a
  ";;"-separated filter LIST to QFileDialog::setNameFilter(), which is
  singular and does not split on ";;", so the type combo showed the raw
  string as one garbled entry and only the trailing glob was ever in force;
  and it handed the line edit's FILE path to setDirectory(), which roots
  Qt's own widget dialog at a non-directory and lists nothing at all. Both
  were masked by the native macOS/Windows dialogs. Also drops the trailing
  ";;" from two getOpenFileName() filters, which rendered as a blank row.
- Linux: File > Open and the colour picker no longer map as an empty frame
  that never paints. FrmKameMain::processSignals() -- the timeout slot of a
  zero-interval QTimer -- slept 5 ms whenever the STM signal buffer was idle,
  and Qt's GTK3 platform theme runs those dialogs through gtk_dialog_run() on
  the same GMainContext as Qt's dispatcher, so a blocking zero-interval timer
  kept GLib from ever reaching GDK's lower-priority redraw source. The same
  pacing is now expressed as the timer's interval, so the thread blocks in
  poll() and the other sources get their turn.
- Every header in the tree now compiles on its own. 38 of them used
  fixed-width integer types without including <cstdint> and parsed only
  because older libstdc++ leaked it through <string> -- newer ones do not,
  and rubywrapper.h's `using Value = uintptr_t` then takes the whole Ruby
  bridge down. <cstdint> now comes from the two prelude headers (kame/
  support.h and kamestm/support.h) with the local include kept only where the
  include graph does not reach a prelude.
- Building the tree with clang for the first time found three defects GCC did
  not: XDSOWithRealtimeAcq's raw-stream reader sized two variable-length
  arrays straight from a channel count read out of the stream, so a truncated
  or corrupt recorded .dat was an unbounded stack allocation (now a fixed
  bound plus validation, which also lets the header compile on MSVC); a
  bitwise | where a || was meant in pulserdriverh8; and an indeterminate int
  copied by a lambda capture in usertempcontrol.

