* Fri Jul 31 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- The field freezes reported while operating T1Mode during an NMR
  measurement, and the SIGABRTs that ended some of them, are fixed. One
  principle explains the whole set: privilege is the STM's completion
  guarantee, and nothing may be immune to it. Two immunities existed and both
  are gone.
- (1) STM-HIGHEST is retired from KAME. AcquisitionPriority now keeps only
  the OS-level elevation, which is a thread property rather than a
  transaction property. A HIGHEST commit never parks, and therefore also
  never yields to a privileged peer -- so each of its commits landing inside
  that peer's closure re-runs the whole closure. Where closures are
  microseconds this is negligible; where they are milliseconds, as KAME's
  analysis closures are, the privilege holder stops converging and everything
  waiting behind it waits through every re-run (reproduced in the lab as 1.1
  -> 15.5 closure re-runs per commit). The tier remains in kamestm for hosts
  that satisfy HIGHEST rate x longest peer closure << 1.
- (2) The wait budget's expiry no longer bypasses fair mode. Its escapes and
  clamps are now gated on the same fair_mode_blocks_me the ordinary path
  consults, so a budget cannot barge past a live privilege holder. Before the
  fix the field freeze reproduced in the lab as 372 HANG dumps and 12.5 s
  pins; after it, none. The budget's other bounds are unchanged.
- The HANG watchdog reports in release builds and no longer aborts
  (KAME_STM_HANG_ABORT_N defaults to 0 there, 3 in debug where the core dump
  is the point). It was tuned for true deadlocks and twice executed
  recoverable states instead: an 11 s self-recovery had 4 s of margin on it,
  and a live privilege holder legitimately grinding for 33 s was killed with
  the unsaved measurement. The starvation bound is now the exit -- 10 s, and
  arming after 2 retries rather than 8, because a thread already sunk in 5 s
  sleep caps accrues retries at one per cap and eight of them deferred the
  exit to ~40 s. The revocable tiers (LOWEST / UI_DEFERRABLE / SCRIPTING) get
  an exception with a message telling the operator to save; NORMAL and
  HIGHEST privilege never expires, by design.
- Constructor exception safety in the base machinery, which the starvation
  throw made reachable and which was a latent double-free before it. XNode's
  constructor pushes shared_ptr(this) onto a per-thread stack so that
  constructors can use shared_from_this(), and createOrphan() pops the back
  -- a positional hand-off that assumed every constructor completes. When one
  throws, `new T` has already run the base destructor and freed the memory
  while the pushed entry still owns it: popping it double-frees, leaving it
  hands the next createOrphan() freed memory. Node constructors run
  transactions by design, so this was live for every driver. All three sites
  (XNode::createOrphan, XQConnectorHolder_, XStatusPrinter::create) now
  neutralise stale entries and verify the pop by identity; Snapshot and
  Transaction constructors drop their own stamps before rethrowing, so a
  throw during construction can no longer orphan a Reserved stamp that every
  negotiator then waits behind. New tests/xnode_ctorthrow_test.
- NMR: the PNR (LS-MDL) background solve is memoized, so an iterate_commit
  retry costs microseconds instead of repeating a 10-20 ms solve. The key is
  provenance -- the DSO record's time() plus the scalar inputs -- rather than
  a content hash, which also reuses the solve across re-analyses triggered by
  unrelated settings; the slot is an atomic_shared_ptr, so there is no mutex
  anywhere near a transaction.
* Thu Jul 30 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- NMR, four problems found setting up a two-site T-sweep: RelaySettleTime
  [ms] on XAutoLCTuner (default 500) replaces a hard-coded 50 ms wait, so a
  sweep that switches the pulser back on the instant tuning() clears no
  longer fires the first pulse into a still-selected VNA path and reads it
  back as a bogus FWD/BWD spike; the tuner hand-off, preset recording and a
  hang on quit are fixed with it.
- NMR/Thamway: the PROT status poll no longer writes a stale reading over a
  fresh setting. fetchStatus reads every setting in one batch of USB round
  trips and must drop the interface lock before its status transaction, so a
  GUI or script write can land in between -- the device was already
  programmed, but the poll then stored its pre-write reading into the node
  and left the two disagreeing until the next poll (observed as PROT.Freq
  showing 17.99 for one poll period after a script wrote 17.36043).
- NMR: the avgClear() touch moved out of analyze() into visualize(). Firing
  it inside the analysis transaction published a nested commit that restarts
  the DSO sequence over the instrument interface -- a side effect the
  enclosing transaction cannot roll back, and one that ran again on every
  retry because the guard flag rolled back with it.
- The record path declares a 20 ms wait budget by default, at every priority
  (XPrimaryDriver::downstreamWaitBudgetUS()). Measurement quality suffers
  past 20 ms whether or not the thread is elevated, so this is not gated on
  the tier. Realtime ends with the record: the demoted downstream work runs
  at NORMAL under the same budget.
- Transactional::isInTransaction() and KAME's gWarnIfInTransaction() replace
  the ad-hoc detectors: a debug build now reports msecsleep() or a mutex
  acquired inside an in-flight transaction, deduplicated by call site, which
  is the deadlock class the driver-authoring rules warn about.
- kame/script: the Python downcaster cache no longer votes in the fallback
  election. cast_to_pyobject elects the applicable downcaster with the
  largest serial, which is correct only because declaration order puts a
  derived class after its base -- cached resolutions carry no declaration
  order, and the trivial cache left behind by a type with no registered base
  (every XScalarEntry read, constantly) outranked every real declaration.
- Interface I/O inside a transaction is now caught where all of it is
  visible, and the audit script sees it one call away; QIODevice::open
  results are no longer discarded; tools/audit gained a guard for
  kamepoolalloc's no-DCAS invariant (which had been passing vacuously on
  macOS) after pointer-width RT counters restored the 32-bit lock-free
  property.

* Tue Jul 28 2026 Kentaro Kitagawa <kitag@issp.u-tokyo.ac.jp>
- The embedded IPython kernel works on ipykernel 7. Kernel.do_one_iteration
  is gone there, and simply silencing the AttributeError traded it for a
  worse failure: Timer.start() then entered its own loop and never returned,
